This README serves as an instroduction to the collocation codes that
Gerrit Holl added to atmlab in June 2010. It serves to point to the most
important m-files as a starting point, and contains information on how to add
support for a new satellite/sensor-pair. For more exhaustive documentation,
see the online help for each of those m-files.

If anything doesn't work or is unclear, e-mail Gerrit at gerrit.holl@ltu.se

HOW TO SET UP THE CONFIGURATION

The collocation settings are in 'colloc_config', which is similar to 'atmlab'.
System-dependent settings are:
- dataset_basedir   root for files from this dataset
- dataset_yeardir   naming pattern for each year for this dataset
...for all datasets you consider

Other settings you might want to change:
- stdout            fid to log output messages
- stderr            fid to log error messages
- collocation_cpr_poes_basedir
  collocation_poes_poes_basedir
                    where to store the collocations
- distance          maximum distance to consider collocation
- interval          maximum time to consider collocation
- POES_satellites_* change this if you don't have MHS/AMSUB for all sats
- no_*              change this if you for some reason don't want data/meandata

There are other settings that should not need change, such as those ending in
_regexp. Those are based on the names from the NOAA CLASS archive, and need to
be changed only if your system uses different names, as well as settings for
internal usage that are mainly there because they're globals.

HOW TO FIND COLLOCATIONS FOR EXISTING SENSOR PAIRS

Use collocate_and_store_date_range to get and store collocations for a range
of dates: a week, a month, a year. Since this is a large amount of data, it
will not be returned, but stored in files per day. Then later, a selection of
those collocations can be read as described below (one can also select all,
but you're likely to run out of memory).

Example use of collocate_and_store_date_range:

>> collocate_and_store_date_range([2009 1 1], [2009 12 31], 'cloudsat', 'cpr', 'noaa18', 'mhs')

This will collect collocations for a full year and write them to the disk. The
location it's written to depends on configuration settings in colloc_config.
The location of the source files to read are also in colloc_config. By default
those are as for the SAT-cluster.

If one wants results directly, without storing them to the disk, one can get
at most one date at the time. In this case, use collocate_date:

Example use of collocate_date:

>> [O D M] = collocate_date(2010, 2, 1, 'cloudsat', 'cpr', 'noaa18', 'mhs')
(...logging output...)
O = 

    noaa18: [173253x29 double]


D = 

    noaa18: [173253x45 double]


M = 

    noaa18: [3890x8 double]

Columns are documented at http://satpage/docs/datasets/collocations/ and
defined in colloc_config('cols_cpr_mhs') with the fields overlap, data and
meandata.

CAUTION! If doubles exist in the original data. they will REMAIN in the
collocations. So when collocating with POES, there WILL be doubles. There is
no functionality in atmlab yet to get rid of those!

SUPPORTED COLLOCATION PAIRS

When collocating, the secondary satellite can be 'poes' which is short for
noaa15–noaa19 + metopa.

- cloudsat cpr vs. poes mhs/amsub

- poes mhs/amsub vs. poes mhs/amsub

- poes hirscs vs. poes mhs/amsub

HIRSCS data is available for a different range of poes than mhs/amsub, and the
satellite names are abbreviated. Hence, the satellite name for hirscs should
be the abbreviated satellite name as it is stored in the filesystem.
This is likely to change in the near future!

RAW COLLOCATIONS

The raw, low-level function to collocate is called 'collocate'. This takes
vectors for time, lat and lon.

HOW TO READ COLLOCATIONS PREVIOUSLY LOCATED

To read previously written collocations, use 'collocation_read'. You give it a
pair of satellite/sensor-couples, a date range and the names of the columns to
read (overlap, data, meandata), as well as a structure defining the
limitations to those that you want to read. for example, to read the latitude,
longitude and distance (mhs/cpr), along with MHS-channels 3–5 and the mean
ROIWP, but only within 20 degrees of the equator, one would write:

>> [M, cols] = collocation_read('cloudsat', 'cpr', 'noaa18', 'mhs', [2008 1 12], [2008 1 14], {{'C_LAT', 'C_LONG', 'B_DIST'}, {'MHS', 3:5}, {'MEAN'}}, struct('C_LAT', [-20 20]));
18-Jun-2010 17:25:29.719:read_collocs_data_mean:36:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/12/collocations_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:29.984:read_collocs_data_mean:42:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/12/collocations_data_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:30.320:read_collocs_data_mean:48:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/12/collocations_meandata_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:30.367:collocation_read:80:7103 -> 1576 collocations
18-Jun-2010 17:25:30.372:read_collocs_data_mean:36:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/13/collocations_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:30.718:read_collocs_data_mean:42:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/13/collocations_data_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:31.123:read_collocs_data_mean:48:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/13/collocations_meandata_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:31.166:collocation_read:80:8776 -> 1865 collocations
18-Jun-2010 17:25:31.170:read_collocs_data_mean:36:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/14/collocations_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:31.271:read_collocs_data_mean:42:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/14/collocations_data_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:31.351:read_collocs_data_mean:48:Reading ~/data/collocations/2008/01/14/collocations_meandata_noaa18.mat
18-Jun-2010 17:25:31.356:collocation_read:80:1888 -> 412 collocations

or, 
>> [M, cols] = collocation_read(...
    'noaa16', 'amsub', 'noaa15', 'amsub', ... % sat/sensor pairs
    [2001 1 1], [2001 1 15], ... % period
    {{'LAT1', 'LONG1', 'C1', 'C2', 'DIST', 'INT'}, ... % geometrical info
     {'AMSU_B1', 3:5, 'AMSU_B2', 3:5}}, ... % actual measurements
     struct(...
        'LAT1', [65 90], ... % latitude between 65 and 90 (inclusive) (only polar)
        'C1', [40 50], ...   % noaa16 near-nadir (columns 40–50)
        'C2', [40 50], ...   % noaa15 near-nadir (columns 40–50)
        'DIST', [0 5], ...   % max 5 km
        'INT', [0 300], ...  % max 5 minutes
        'AMSU_B1', [0 400], ... % all requested NOAA-16 chans unflagged
        'AMSU_B2', [0 400]);    % all requested NOAA-15 chans unflagged
(output omitted)
>> whos
  Name        Size            Bytes  Class     Attributes

  M         446x12            42816  double              
  cols        1x1              1504  struct              
>> plot(...                    % make a scatter plot...
    M(:, cols.AMSU_B1(3)), ... % of NOAA-16/AMSU-B channel 20 ...
    M(:, cols.AMSU_B2(3)), ... % versus NOAA-15/AMSU-B channel 20. ..
    'k.')

More information in the help of 'collocation_read'.

HOW TO ADD A NEW SET OF SENSORS

To add a new pair of sensors, the collocation system needs to know:
- how to find all data for a particular date
- how long one granule file takes
- how to read the granule files

If all source datasets are already supported in existing pairs, steps 1–4 can
be skipped.

1.
In colloc_config, define where the data can be found, if it is not there
yet. You need to define a base directory in SENSOR_basedir.

WARNING! When colloc_config is edited, all values are reset to default. If any
collocations are running, this will cause problems!

For most datasets, data are stored per day, at least on the SAT cluster.
If this is the case, one needs to define, besides SENSOR_basedir,
SENSOR_yeardir and SENSOR_regexp. The latter is a regular expression matched
against all filenames in the directory for the particular day.
find_datafile_by_date will then return the filename for the first match, whereas
find_granules_by_date will use the named matches for 'hour' and 'minute'
to return [year month day hour minute] for each of the days.

For example, for AVHRR, I added:

A.avhrr_basedir = '/storage3/data/avhrr';
A.avhrr_yeardir = '$SAT_gac_$YEAR';
A.avhrr_regexp = '\.S(?<hour>\d{2})(?<minute>\d{2})\.';

Alternately, the data may not be stored per day, such as is the case for
hirscs. In that case, it has to be one granule per day, and one needs to
link a function that will calculates the sole granule for that day. The
yeardir should be empty, as this is used to check whether to assume data
sorted per day. For example, for hirscs, I added:

A.hirscs_basedir = '/storage3/data/hirscs';
A.hirscs_yeardir = '';
A.hirscs_getpath = @colloc_get_filename_hirscs;

Then, the path will be:

fullfile(hirscs_basedir, colloc_get_filename_hircs(year, month, day, sat))

Maybe in some cases, neither of the above situations applies, although the
latter approach should be quite flexible. Those cases are not currently
supported, but will be supported when needed.

2.
In colloc_config, define how long a granule takes. For example, for hirscs,
I added:

A.granule_duration_hirscs = 86400; % one granule per day

3. In colloc_config, define configuration variables describing what m-file
should be used to read each of the datasets. For the POES/POES example, this
was already there, because POES are read for CloudSat/MHS-collocations as
well. For reference, those lines are like:

A.read_mhs = @colloc_read_poes_radiometer;


4. Create the m-files just linked to. In this case,
colloc_read_poes_radiometer already existed because CloudSat/MHS-collocations
use this. All readers should take a string as an input, the path to the file
to read, and should output a structure with at least the fields 'lat', 'lon'
and 'time'. One probably wants to add some more information for
post-collocating processing, see step 8. See existing readers for examples.


3. In colloc_config, define configuration variables describing the location of
where the collocations should be stored. For example, to add POES/POES, I added:

A.collocation_poes_poes_basedir = '~/data/collocations_poes_poes';
A.collocation_poes_poes_yeardir = '$YEAR';
A.collocation_poes_poes_filename = 'collocations_$SAT1_$SAT2.mat';
A.collocation_poes_poes_data_filename = 'collocations_data_$SAT1_$SAT2.mat';

Those can be redefined at a later stage, and the function calculating the
filename (see step3) should thus use those rather than hardcoded paths.

4. In colloc_config, define configuration variables describing what m-file
should be used to calculate the filename from year/month/day/sat1/sat2. For
example, for POES/POES, I added:

A.filename_poes_poes = @colloc_get_path_poes_poes;

Note that 'mhs' and 'amsub' are aliases for 'poes'.

5. Create the m-files just linked to. The existing colloc_get_path_* can serve
as an example, It should take 5 input arguments (year, month, day, sat1, sat2)
and give up to 3 output arguments (path for collocations, path for
collocation data and path for mean collocation data). Here, you should use the
variables stored in step 1 and replace strings like $SAT1 and $SAT2 with
appropiate names using a function like strrep. Because colloc_config is
configurable, the user can deviate from default paths and naming patterns.

6. In colloc_config, define configuration variables describing what m-file
should be used to process the collocations. After collocating, one probably
wants to store some more data along with the collocations, like brightness
temperatures, so that one doesn't need to return to the original files all the
time. You also need to define the number of outputs.
For example, for POES/POES, I added:

A.process_poes_poes = @colloc_process_poes_poes;

A.process_data_poes_poes = @colloc_process_data_poes_poes;

A.no_poes_poes = 2;

Note that 'mhs' and 'amsub' are aliases for 'poes'.

7. In colloc_config, define the columns of the matrices one will create in
step 8 after the processing. In processing, one wants to collect information
such as brightness temperatures. Those are structures that should define each
column by name, and also give the total number of columns. This has to be of
the name cols_sensor_sensor (where mhs/amsub are synonyms to poes) because of
automated conversion between column names and numbers. For poes/poes, I added:


A.cols_poes_poes.overlap = struct(...
    'LAT1', 1, ...
    'LONG1', 2, ...
    'START1', 3, ...
    'TIME1', 4, ...
    'I1', 5, ...
    'C1', 6, ...
    'LAT2', 7, ...
    'LONG2', 8, ...
    'START2', 9, ...
    'TIME2', 10, ...
    'I2', 11, ...
    'C2', 12, ...
    'DIST', 13, ...
    'INT', 14, ...
    'NCOLS', 14);

A.cols_poes_poes.data = struct(...
    'AMSU_B1', 1:5, ...
    'MHS1', 1:5, ...
    'AMSU_B2', 6:10, ...
    'MHS2', 6:10, ...
    'NCOLS', 10);

Be sure to add 'NCOLS', because this is required by some places.

8. Create the m-files linked to in step 6. The existing colloc_process_* can serve
as examples. One can have up to three processing files for each pair of
sensors: process_sensor1_sensor2, process_data_sensor1_sensor2 and
process_meandata_sensor1_sensor2. They all have a predefined interface. For
process_sensor1_sensor2, input arguments are collocations, name1, date1,
data1, name2, date2, data2, where collocations will be the output of
'collocate', namen the name of the satellite, daten the datevec for the
starting time, and datan the output of the read-function as defined in
function 5 (thus there one wants to add more information). For process_data,
there is an additional argument (position 2) having the output of process_.
For process_meandata, there are two additional arguments (positions 2 and 3)
having the output of the earlier two. See existing examples.

At this stage, we can collocate and store collocatons as follows:

>> collocate_and_store_date_range([2010 1 1], [2010 1 3], 'metopa', 'mhs', 'noaa18', 'mhs')



In case of problems, please send mail to gerrit.holl@ltu.se.
